      "num": 2,
      "title": "The Chariot of Fire and the Double Portion",
      "summary": "When the Lord is about to take Elijah up to heaven by a whirlwind, Elijah tries three times to leave Elisha behind — at Gilgal, Bethel, and Jericho — and three times Elisha vows, 'As the LORD liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not leave thee.' At each stop the sons of the prophets ask Elisha whether he knows his master is being taken today, and he answers, 'Yea, I know it; hold ye your peace.' At the Jordan, as fifty prophets watch from a distance, Elijah rolls up his mantle, strikes the water, and the two cross on dry ground. Elijah asks what he can do for his successor, and Elisha asks for a double portion of his spirit; a hard thing, Elijah says, but it will be his if he sees him taken. Suddenly a chariot of fire and horses of fire part them, Elijah goes up by a whirlwind, and Elisha cries, 'My father, my father, the chariot of Israel, and the horsemen thereof,' tearing his clothes in two. He picks up the fallen mantle, strikes the Jordan asking 'Where is the LORD God of Elijah?', and the waters part for him too. At Jericho he heals the bad spring with salt from a new bowl, and near Bethel a gang of youths jeering 'Go up, thou bald head' is mauled by two she-bears — sobering signs that Elijah's God now stands behind Elisha.",
