      "historianNote": "Dothan is Tell Dothan, a large mound about ten miles north of Samaria guarding a pass on the road from the Jezreel Valley into the hill country — the same route Joseph's brothers used (Genesis 37:17). Joseph Free's excavations there (1953–1964) found substantial Iron Age occupation, including a 9th-century town, confirming it as a fortified settlement on exactly the kind of strategic road a Syrian raiding force would want to control. The axe head matters because iron tools were still costly in the 9th century; a borrowed one meant a real debt for a poor prophet. During the siege, eighty shekels for an ass's head — an unclean animal's least edible part — and five shekels for a quarter cab (roughly a third of a litre) of 'dove's dung' are deliberate famine-economics figures: a slave's price in Exodus 21:32 was thirty shekels. Whether 'dove's dung' is literal (bought for fuel or salt, as Josephus suggests) or a folk name for a wild plant such as carob pods or Star-of-Bethlehem bulbs is debated, and the cannibalism fulfils the covenant curse of Deuteronomy 28:53–57, a curse also found in Assyrian treaty texts.",
