      "historianNote": "The 'river of Egypt to the river Euphrates' (24:7) reflects the Battle of Carchemish in 605 BC, when the crown prince Nebuchadnezzar routed Neco's army on the Euphrates (compare Jeremiah 46:2), after which Babylon claimed all of Syria and Palestine; the Babylonian Chronicle also records a bruising Babylonian-Egyptian clash in 601 BC, which may have tempted Jehoiakim to rebel. The same Chronicle (British Museum tablet BM 21946) gives one of the most precise dates in the Old Testament: in his seventh year, on the second day of the month Adar — 15/16 March 597 BC — Nebuchadnezzar 'seized the city of Judah, captured the king, appointed a king of his own choice, took heavy tribute and brought it to Babylon,' matching Jehoiachin's surrender and Zedekiah's appointment (the Bible's 'eighth year' counts the regnal year differently). Ezekiel, a priest among these 597 deportees, dates his prophecies by 'king Jehoiachin's captivity' (Ezekiel 1:2), showing that many exiles still regarded Jehoiachin as the legitimate king. Jehoiakim's death just before the siege is not described in Kings, and Jeremiah's grim prediction of 'the burial of an ass' (Jeremiah 22:19) has been read different ways.",
