      "summary": "In Zedekiah's ninth year Nebuchadnezzar besieges Jerusalem, and after eighteen months the famine is so severe there is no bread for the people of the land. The wall is breached; Zedekiah and his soldiers flee by night through the gate between two walls by the king's garden, but the Chaldeans overtake him in the plains of Jericho, his army scatters, and he is brought to Nebuchadnezzar at Riblah. There they slay Zedekiah's sons before his eyes, put out his eyes, bind him in fetters of brass, and carry him to Babylon. A month later Nebuzaradan, captain of the guard, burns the house of the Lord, the king's house, and every great house in Jerusalem, breaks down the walls, carries away the bronze pillars and vessels, and leads the people into exile; the chief priests and officials are executed at Riblah — 'So Judah was carried away out of their land.' Nebuchadnezzar appoints Gedaliah son of Ahikam governor over the poor left behind, and he urges them from Mizpah to serve the king of Babylon and live; but Ishmael, of the royal seed, murders him, and the remnant flees to Egypt for fear of the Chaldeans. The book does not end there: in the thirty-seventh year of Jehoiachin's captivity, Evil-merodach king of Babylon lifts up his head out of prison, speaks kindly to him, sets his throne above the other captive kings, and Jehoiachin eats bread continually before him all the days of his life.",
