      "historianNote": "The final siege is illuminated by the Lachish Letters, twenty-one inked ostraca found by James Starkey in 1935 in a gatehouse destruction layer at Lachish; in Letter 4 an officer writes that 'we are watching for the signal-fires of Lachish... for we cannot see Azekah,' strikingly matching Jeremiah 34:7, when Lachish and Azekah were the last fortified cities standing. In Jerusalem the Babylonian destruction of 586 BC (some date it 587) is vivid in the City of David — the 'Burnt Room,' the House of Ahiel, collapsed buildings, Scythian-type arrowheads, and clay bullae baked hard by the fire that consumed the archive that held them. A bulla from Lachish reads 'belonging to Gedaliah who is over the house,' and many identify him with Gedaliah son of Ahikam before his governorship, though the name was common and the link is not certain; Mizpah is generally identified with Tell en-Nasbeh, which, unusually, shows no destruction and continued occupation into the Babylonian period, fitting its role as Gedaliah's capital. Most remarkably, cuneiform ration tablets excavated near the Ishtar Gate in Nebuchadnezzar's Babylon and published by Ernst Weidner in 1939 list oil allotted to 'Ya'u-kinu king of the land of Yahudu' and his five sons around 592 BC — Jehoiachin alive and fed by the Babylonian court. Evil-merodach is Amel-Marduk, Nebuchadnezzar's son, who reigned only from 562 to 560 BC, which dates Jehoiachin's release to about 561/560 BC.",
